 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$, where $N$ is the number of pixels or voxels, as it requires a single pass to compute differences and averages [S6, S10, unofficial].
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for storing intermediate squared differences if not computed in-place, or $O(1)$ if computed incrementally [S6, S10, unofficial].
-- **Performance:** Fast and efficient, suitable for real-time logging during training [S2, S9, unofficial].
-- **Strengths:** Simple to implement, differentiable, and widely understood as a standard metric [S1, S4, unofficial].
-- **Limitations:** Does not account for structural information or human perception, potentially leading to visually suboptimal results despite low MSE [S1, S2, unofficial]. Sensitive to outliers due to squaring [S11, unofficial].
-- **When to Use:** As a primary loss function for training when photometric fidelity is the main goal, or as a standard evaluation metric [S4, S7, unofficial].
-- **When Not to Use:** When structural similarity or perceptual quality is more important than pixel-wise accuracy [S1, S2, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$, where $N$ is the number of pixels or voxels. The operation is element-wise and parallelizable.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ to store the squared differences or the final MSE/PSNR scalar if computed incrementally.
+*   **Performance:** Computationally inexpensive compared to structural metrics like SSIM or LPIPS. It is the standard loss for initial convergence in NeRF and volume rendering training due to its simplicity and differentiability [S4, unofficial][S7, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Simple to implement, differentiable, provides a clear global error signal, widely used as a baseline metric [S1, unofficial][S4, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Does not correlate well with human perception for high-frequency details or structural fidelity; sensitive to outliers; can lead to blurry results if used alone without perceptual or structural losses [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When to Use:** Standard photometric supervision, baseline evaluation, scenarios where color accuracy is paramount and structural details are less critical or handled by other losses.
+*   **When Not to Use:** When perceptual quality is the primary goal (use SSIM/LPIPS), or when dealing with significant structural misalignments (use IoU/Depth losses).
 
 ## Background
-MSE and PSNR are standard metrics in image and signal processing, widely adopted in differentiable volume rendering for evaluating reconstruction quality [S1, S2, unofficial]. MSE was introduced as a fundamental error metric in early computer vision and graphics literature, while PSNR became a standard benchmark for image compression and reconstruction quality [S1, S2, unofficial]. In the context of Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) and related methods, MSE is the default loss function for optimizing radiance fields, as demonstrated in the original NeRF paper and subsequent works [S4, S7, unofficial]. PSNR is commonly used in papers to report quantitative results, providing a standardized way to compare different methods [S1, S2, unofficial].
+MSE is a fundamental statistical measure of error. In the context of volume rendering and NeRF, it was popularized by the original NeRF paper [S2, unofficial] as the primary loss function for optimizing the radiance field. It has since become the standard photometric loss in various 3D reconstruction and novel view synthesis pipelines [S1, unofficial][S4, unofficial][S7, unofficial]. PSNR is a standard metric in image processing, adapted for 3D reconstruction evaluation to provide a comparable scale across different scenes and resolutions [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
 
